# Line endings, with one case that actually matters.
#
# These files are executed by a Linux shell on the VM. A clone made on Windows with
# `core.autocrlf=true` would otherwise give them CRLF endings, and a shebang with a trailing
# carriage return fails as `bash\r: no such file or directory` — a twenty-minute mystery during a
# deploy. `text eol=lf` pins them whatever the cloning machine is configured to do.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repository and leave the working copy to the platform.
*               text=auto

# Never touched, never diffed as text.
*.xlsx          binary
*.png           binary
*.ico           binary
*.gz            binary

# Generated. Marking it keeps a lock refresh out of a diff review, but it is still committed and
# still reviewed when a dependency actually changes.
uv.lock             linguist-generated=true
frontend/package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
